        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>Do you need a lot of storage? Well, then, you\u2019re going to have to pay for it\u2014and you\u2019ll be paying a lot more than you would have a year ago, as <a href=\"https://www.pcworld.com/article/3028843/dram-ssd-shortages-could-last-months-to-years-vendors-say.html\">\u201cAI\u201d data centers gobble up</a> a bigger and bigger share of the industry\u2019s output of memory and storage. But even the most lavish among us would probably wince at dropping nearly $16,000 USD on an M.2 drive, even if it does have a capacity of 16 terabytes.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Exascend-Enterprise-Grade-PCIe-Gen4-NVMe/dp/B0FWKDR1JC/?tag=pcworld02-20&#038;asc_refurl=https://www.pcworld.com\">Exascend Enterprise-Grade PE4 Gen 4 SSD</a> now comes in a 16TB capacity, and as far as I can tell, it\u2019s the first one to hit that mark for a single M.2 drive sold to the public at a standard re",
